  "summary": "A Tibetan Buddhist ritual art form involving the creation and destruction of intricate mandalas made of colored sand.",
  "description": "Mandalas are created by monks using metal funnels called chak-pur, pouring millions of grains of sand to form intricate geometric patterns and deities. This ephemeral art represents impermanence and is destroyed in a closing ceremony, with sand distributed to bless the environment.",
  "culturalContext": "Tibetan mandala sand painting — colored-sand cosmic diagrams laid grain by grain (dul-tson-kyil-khor) by monastery teams over days or weeks — visualizes deity palaces (Kalachakra's 723 deities) then is ritual-ly destroyed: swept and poured into flowing water as impermanence teaching. Documented globally since the Dalai Lama's 1988 tours (the Kalachakra sand mandala at New York's Tibet House), the practice is Tibetan Buddhism's most cinematic ritual and the impermanence aesthetic's supreme demonstration.",
